<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Giovanni Malatesta</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1240–<a href="/years/1304">1304</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Gianciotto Malatesta</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Giovanni Malatesta, known as Gianciotto, was a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malatesta-da-verucchio">Malatesta da Verucchio</a>, lord of Rimini, and served as podestà of Pesaro. Married for political reasons to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francesca-da-rimini">Francesca da Polenta</a> of Ravenna, he killed her and his brother Paolo around 1285 after discovering their love affair. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dante-alighieri">Dante</a>'s Inferno, Francesca foretells that he will be punished in Caina, among the traitors to their kin.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/giovanni-malatesta.jpg" alt="Giovanni Malatesta" width="320" height="446" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jean-Auguste-Dominique Ingres · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gianciotto_Discovers_Paolo_and_Francesca_Jean_Auguste_Dominique_Ingres.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
